		 <td>                            
	                                   

<P>&#9;Here was the manifestation of the shaman's power.  I had not seen it so closely before.  The animal spirits were life.   They were here, virile and strong, filling Norrath with their voices, long before the first seed of man.  A chill ran down my spine as I watched Kheldan call upon them, focus them, and direct them--to give their strength to Gnasher.</P><br><br><P>&#9;Outside the birds were singing.  It was spring.</P><br><P>&#9;</P><br><P>&#9;Only a short time better, Gnasher's chest moved easily under the blankets and his old, furry face had relaxed into a visage of restful sleep.</P><br><P>&#9;Kheldan slumped next to the bed, half sitting on the floor, exhausted.</P><br><P>&#9;"Thank you, Kheldan"</P><br><P>&#9;He nodded weakly in response and smiled, "It was the right thing.  I am glad I did it."</P><br><P>&#9;"Are you alright?"</P><br><P>&#9;He laughed softly, "I'm spent.  Yer friend is a tough one.  His spirit is like old leather.  Is there anything to eat here?  I'm starving."</P><br><P>&#9;I laughed.  The relief in my voice I'm sure was palpable.  "I'll clean up a bit and make a fire.  It's a mess, but I'm sure there is plenty.</P><br><br><P>&#9;With a strange elation, I stepped to the work, trying to put the shambles of the place back together.      What was that elation?  It was a strange sense of power. </P><br><br><P>&#9;Yes, that is what it was.  Gnasher was dying...almost done with the act of it.  But we had come and saved his life. </P><br><P> It was not the helpless feeling of my blurry memory, gazing up into the dark, watching Layla in the claws of the creature. </P><DIR><br><DIR><br><br><P> It was not helpless. </P></DIR><br></DIR><br><br><P>&#9;The woodpile was around the side of the house, and also a heavy axe.  The heft of it felt good as my shoulders rolled easily in the act of splitting the wood.  Work was good.</P><br><P>&#9;Work helped me think.</P><br><P>&#9;My debt to Kheldan was immeasurable.  Were it not for him, I'd be digging Gnasher's grave right now and not cutting wood for a fire.  So inconceivable was it to me that the laws of Antonius Bayle would demand Gnasher's head.  He was a threat to no one.</P><br><P>&#9;The axe thudded to rest in the chopping block and the split log fell obediently away on either side.</P><br><P>&#9;Gnasher's head....</P><br><P>&#9;Time seemed to slow for a moment, as I watched the clean cut piece of wood topple off the block, lazily to the ground.</P><br><P>&#9;…Gnasher’s head was still quite solidly attached to his shoulders….</P><br><br><P>&#9;It was spring....</P><br><br><P>&#9;I heard the hollow sound of the wood split  as it bounced on the earth.</P><br><br><P>&#9;The birds had stopped their singing...</P><br><br><P>&#9;They weren't after Gnasher…</P><br><br><P>&#9;Suddenly, my body was a live wire and every hair stood on end.  I whirled, tearing the axe from the wood without effort.  The thick oak of its haft whirled with me.</P><br><br><P>&#9;And he was there.</P><br><br><P>&#9;An elf...lithe and dark of skin.  His straight, brown hair tied back from his face.  He was cloaked in brown and clad in green the green and black patchwork so reputed to make the people f Kelethin unseen in the woods.  The silvery arc of his tomahawk whistled through the air arcing around at me head...</P><br><br><P>&#9;...and thudded solidly stuck in the wall of the cabin.&#9;</P><br><br><P>&#9;In that frozen moment of time I looked into his green eyes.  They were the hard, even eyes of a warrior.</P><br><br><P>&#9;"You missed." </P>         <br>
